#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128862 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129284 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "89f280af-9947-5d54-9284-9ae75d46c682" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Moment of Inertia Given a Description of a Mass Density Function"}] :question/id #uuid "0a99e46a-2104-5285-862b-96b98e7a2f85" :question/math-academy-id "e-12482" :question/problem "![](images/0c/0cfd94391c0090bc628e4203af3ff15ff7407c9ecc4cc4e7a6f6ac7a1cf36b25.png)\n\nA triangular plate of mass ![](images/34/34058a2a5d1de088feed7d9825ba218394c9a8bb880b046be7b47307cdd37ce1.svg) occupies the region ![](images/59/5914524b1d3db63c66998b75ca1d00a5dc4e294d2a8f10fe384d2b2ebf2af38e.svg) in the first quadrant enclosed by the lines ![](images/23/238e977be23f692486c8bf68f6b40255435b3d1796cc50d260a014a8c670be7b.svg) and ![](images/36/36fdd3a0fb618d9692c52fd9bcb6e916441dee88dbce405fb885ec0f7782f047.svg) and the ![](images/fc/fcc5df999e3b49fae1685ee0ab5bd03709804e1dd49f2ee722c4269eb0f5506d.svg)-axis. The mass density at each point is proportional to its distance from the ![](images/fc/fcc5df999e3b49fae1685ee0ab5bd03709804e1dd49f2ee722c4269eb0f5506d.svg)-axis. Find the moment of inertia of the plate about the ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-axis." :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina ![](images/59/5914524b1d3db63c66998b75ca1d00a5dc4e294d2a8f10fe384d2b2ebf2af38e.svg) with mass density function ![](images/ac/ac8c3fda52a02808979c7543290abb75797c85b3b0db8aed7a51cc2afda66995.svg) we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the ![](images/fc/fcc5df999e3b49fae1685ee0ab5bd03709804e1dd49f2ee722c4269eb0f5506d.svg)-axis is given by\n![](images/66/66764283bb64f05a967263265ff2c3253049505060577b19b8beb83be225efb9.svg)\n\n- The moment of inertia about the ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-axis is given by\n![](images/f3/f30602db401e65f6fa3e007153a1e2d8ddd35f5ef202bab4118ba2663ec03325.svg)\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin is given by\n![](images/a6/a6f586b23532d418063be8ede925099a9dd47ba598ca4fab8190c4f7c4889595.svg)\n\nFirst, notice that ![](images/59/5914524b1d3db63c66998b75ca1d00a5dc4e294d2a8f10fe384d2b2ebf2af38e.svg) is a type I region with representation\n![](images/66/668bf889423235cf4ad8497796bfd3e50140d258a325a75b84e4a6822b288204.svg)\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/c1/c1301c17b1dbb8b48716d09ad6f0a7a105917ea7a1c0e942bbe087b12772f254.png)\n\nFrom the description, the mass density function can be written as \n![](images/41/4115a69fc623e7ccfc7cfccb7285afc0439a111ae4f2424693481fbf7dcd3c54.svg)\nwhere ![](images/8d/8dc6f7e80ecaac9fa07890ce1881dd51de319597ae8b1dce821aa03c1f3eff06.svg) is a constant of proportionality. \n\nSince the density contains the unknown constant ![](images/8d/8dc6f7e80ecaac9fa07890ce1881dd51de319597ae8b1dce821aa03c1f3eff06.svg), we first use the total mass ![](images/34/34058a2a5d1de088feed7d9825ba218394c9a8bb880b046be7b47307cdd37ce1.svg) to determine ![](images/c7/c7432bf7a520e569e53fdd1a7303ce6b9f940d99e68b5e7ea3196c51acfe2b69.svg)\n\nWe can find an expression for ![](images/8d/8dc6f7e80ecaac9fa07890ce1881dd51de319597ae8b1dce821aa03c1f3eff06.svg) in terms of ![](images/34/34058a2a5d1de088feed7d9825ba218394c9a8bb880b046be7b47307cdd37ce1.svg) by integrating the density function over the plate:\n\n![](images/03/03176dbe18e8d06ce05d43951fd0759d88ab36e651d08de70ce17465da4e9128.svg)\n\nTherefore, ![](images/55/55f2ccc4062bb6cdf9af22e7f89a1a17bf6445718da120e253149a8a314616f3.svg) and hence ![](images/38/3895193c54afc999ca98d66da9ff11ad1747545f8d786e6e1e8630aaf74cfffa.svg) Therefore, the moment of inertia about the ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-axis is\n![](images/e7/e7f8cc88a82c9fec7d20bcf57129dea1d569e1a19ef9bcbe9e6be47e9841d939.svg)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128863 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129285 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eba1f811-7b7d-56f6-8b49-4e7a4c7db81b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Moments of Inertia Using Polar Coordinates"}] :question/id #uuid "dbf6a3b7-abb9-5f62-83af-00169c673bc6" :question/math-academy-id "e-12483" :question/problem "![](images/12/12fb5f07885b074f983cab3a5fba0faff607d8aa34d7cd3abe4e94abf53dac25.png)\n\nConsider the plate that occupies the region $D$ enclosed by the polar circles $r = 1$ and $r = 2$ and the polar rays $\\theta = \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}$ and $\\theta = \\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$ in the first quadrant, as shown above. The mass density function of the plate is given by $\\lambda(x,y) = \\dfrac{1}{x^2}.$ Find the moment of inertia of the plate about the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda(r,\\theta),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $x$-axis, in polar coordinates, is given by\n\n$$\nI_x = \\iint\\limits_D \\lambda(r,\\theta) \\cdot r^3\\sin^2{\\theta} \\:\\textrm{d}r\\:\\textrm{d}\\theta.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $y$-axis, in polar coordinates, is given by\n\n$$\nI_y = \\iint\\limits_D \\lambda(r,\\theta) \\cdot  r^3\\cos^2{\\theta} \\:\\textrm{d}r\\:\\textrm{d}\\theta.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin, in polar coordinates, is given by\n\n$$\nI_0 = \\iint\\limits_D \\lambda(r,\\theta) \\cdot  r^3 \\:\\textrm{d}r\\:\\textrm{d}\\theta = I_x + I_y.\n$$\n\nFirst, note that the region $D$ in polar coordinates can be expressed as\n\n$$\nD = \\left\\{ (r,\\theta) \\: : \\: 1 \\leq r \\leq 2, \\:\\: \\dfrac{\\pi}{6} \\leq \\theta \\leq \\dfrac{\\pi}{4} \\right\\}.\n$$\n\nAlso, the mass density function in polar coordinates is \n\n$$\n\\lambda(r,\\theta) = \\dfrac{1}{r^2 \\cos^2{\\theta}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{y} & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /4}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\cdot {r}^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /4}{\\int }_{1}^{2}r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =({\\int }_{1}^{2}r\\,\\text{d}r)({\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /4}\\text{d}\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{r}^{2}\\,{|}_{1}^{2}\\cdot \\theta \\,{|}_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(4-1)\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{4}-\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{12} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129030 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129211 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "92cc3d27-0e31-571f-9b92-4f209c5a08e0" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Moment of Inertia of a Lamina With Uniform Mass Density"}] :question/id #uuid "c317696a-899d-5af5-9bf4-2d38862f5877" :question/math-academy-id "e-8542" :question/problem "![](images/de/de882e240d0a6ca824e77397fe9c6b51eb94d36c2e5e3524422ef0a0f28103de.png)\n\nA triangular plate of mass ![](images/34/34058a2a5d1de088feed7d9825ba218394c9a8bb880b046be7b47307cdd37ce1.svg) and uniform mass density occupies the region ![](images/59/5914524b1d3db63c66998b75ca1d00a5dc4e294d2a8f10fe384d2b2ebf2af38e.svg) enclosed between the ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-axis and the lines ![](images/b3/b3259a74b1b63f2b49841acd0eea4f599e9dacf9c4f1b17bda0a3cb115a9d90e.svg) and ![](images/fd/fdad9cd601a0652f1c927ea8e805525f51c3a144e924e6eabe5c75d186e3062b.svg) as shown above. Find the moment of inertia of the plate about the ![](images/fc/fcc5df999e3b49fae1685ee0ab5bd03709804e1dd49f2ee722c4269eb0f5506d.svg)-axis." :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina ![](images/59/5914524b1d3db63c66998b75ca1d00a5dc4e294d2a8f10fe384d2b2ebf2af38e.svg) with mass density function ![](images/ac/ac8c3fda52a02808979c7543290abb75797c85b3b0db8aed7a51cc2afda66995.svg) we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the ![](images/fc/fcc5df999e3b49fae1685ee0ab5bd03709804e1dd49f2ee722c4269eb0f5506d.svg)-axis is given by\n![](images/66/66764283bb64f05a967263265ff2c3253049505060577b19b8beb83be225efb9.svg)\n\n- The moment of inertia about the ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-axis is given by\n![](images/f3/f30602db401e65f6fa3e007153a1e2d8ddd35f5ef202bab4118ba2663ec03325.svg)\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin is given by\n![](images/a6/a6f586b23532d418063be8ede925099a9dd47ba598ca4fab8190c4f7c4889595.svg)\n\nFirst, notice that ![](images/59/5914524b1d3db63c66998b75ca1d00a5dc4e294d2a8f10fe384d2b2ebf2af38e.svg) is a type I region with representation\n\n![](images/0b/0b668ee2254c04000cdd2a7f9434e780844366cd2f6f4dbd0798840a688d744e.svg)\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/68/68904cbee1839a61f4d65c5ff03066d8f28b8a5e070f6ddb00c82c29cfd93fcd.png)\n\nThe area of the triangular plate ![](images/59/5914524b1d3db63c66998b75ca1d00a5dc4e294d2a8f10fe384d2b2ebf2af38e.svg) is \n![](images/7f/7fa087c9262341fe08582d9053395cc577a1c441e3ca201619e13deda73c8dc3.svg)\n\nSince the plate has uniform density, every unit of area contains the same amount of mass. Therefore, the mass density is constant and is given by the total mass divided by the total area:\n![](images/4e/4ea37907521bd2d810e88505cf91e44b78cd2400bba8ab67ef244c1cb62bdeaa.svg)\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the ![](images/fc/fcc5df999e3b49fae1685ee0ab5bd03709804e1dd49f2ee722c4269eb0f5506d.svg)-axis is\n\n![](images/ab/ab2f126d6507eb07fb23f834015c981794f3367a2f14b7e43ac688d5a9af803c.svg)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129031 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129212 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3fdc1a2a-8a35-59f2-9890-5b835693df4f" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Moment of Inertia of a Lamina Given a Mass Density Function"}] :question/id #uuid "bee275c6-cca6-515f-b737-597ecca278a6" :question/math-academy-id "e-8543" :question/problem "![](images/46/46d6a57700d1df7f2de9a46507821a8553876eb956be6ef87a455d5ca0fabafb.png)\n\nA plate occupies the region $D$ enclosed by the curves $y=x^2$ and $y=1$ in the first quadrant, as shown above. If the mass density function of $D$ is given by $\\lambda(x,y) = 4xy,$ find the moment of inertia of the lamina about the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda(x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is given by\n\n$$\nI_x =  \\iint\\limits_D y^2\\lambda(x,y)  \\:\\textrm{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is given by\n\n$$\nI_y =  \\iint\\limits_D x^2\\lambda(x,y)  \\:\\textrm{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin is given by\n\n$$\nI_0 = \\iint\\limits_D (x^2+y^2) \\lambda(x,y)  \\:\\textrm{d}A = I_x + I_y.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that $D$ is a type I region with representation\n\n$$\nD = \\left\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 1, \\:\\: x^2 \\leq y \\leq 1 \\right\\},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/88/8846da0fe3b1e8369a9277bbaba1e082d040136a71105a5816af7b13d2788027.png)\n\nUsing the formula for $I_x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{x} & ={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{1}{y}^{2}\\cdot 4xy\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{1}4x{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[x{y}^{4}]_{y={x}^{2}}^{y=1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}x(1-{x}^{8})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}x-{x}^{9}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{x}^{10}}{10}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129532 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129211 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "92cc3d27-0e31-571f-9b92-4f209c5a08e0" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Moment of Inertia of a Lamina With Uniform Mass Density"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a0a8b33a-6039-541e-b58e-1f0a29efa688" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2M}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126690} {:answer/id #uuid "bdc5d6a1-85df-58ec-8f12-88aeac9bf8ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{M}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126691} {:answer/id #uuid "f26bff63-e8ea-527f-8e55-bced519e9384" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "M" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126692} {:answer/id #uuid "18fe4b91-2465-5f90-aa80-4b1a75799c68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{M}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126693} {:answer/id #uuid "27fbd22f-ff3d-5928-ab73-3c683a066225" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3M}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126694}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a0a8b33a-6039-541e-b58e-1f0a29efa688" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2M}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126690} :answer-field/id #uuid "dabbdf32-9b8d-5ae1-a71a-6f12fe91d019" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126689}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f0af98bb-12cf-5c0f-a924-a0fe039adf99" :question/math-academy-id "q-162906" :question/problem "![](images/64/64cadaa8b079c80659d661be55709e572c05b185a6b6fdb60e24801a9d1e84a5.png)\n\nA triangular plate of mass $M$ and uniform mass density occupies the region $D$ enclosed between the $x$-axis and the lines $y=2x$ and $y=3-x,$ as shown above. Find the moment of inertia of the plate about the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A={I}_{x}+{I}_{y}.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that $D$ is a type II region with representation\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,\\,\\frac{y}{2}\\le x\\le 3-y\\},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/bc/bcf045b47856148dc5f7c05a2800804fb34aad2d0755983375c20c35bb00f0e4.png)\n\nThe area of the triangular plate $D$ is \n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 3\\cdot 2=3.\n$$\n\nSince the plate has uniform density, every unit of area contains the same amount of mass. Therefore, the mass density is constant and is given by the total mass divided by the total area:\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{M}{A}=\\frac{M}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{x} & ={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{y/2}^{3-y}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{y}^{2}[{\\int }_{y/2}^{3-y}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{y}^{2}(3-y-\\frac{1}{2}y)\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}3{y}^{2}-\\frac{3}{2}{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{3}{[{y}^{3}-\\frac{3}{8}{y}^{4}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{3}[(8-6)-0] \\\\  & =\\frac{2M}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129534 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129212 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3fdc1a2a-8a35-59f2-9890-5b835693df4f" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Moment of Inertia of a Lamina Given a Mass Density Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8147d1fc-c210-5b6e-8737-184cbc1286bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "48" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126702} {:answer/id #uuid "df3fdcb9-dc5c-5e74-9f19-98291d28ee58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "64" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126703} {:answer/id #uuid "34415bc5-ab17-5a7a-8ce2-b7d4896e7d0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126704} {:answer/id #uuid "5053599a-8d82-5e1e-b8d7-901289aa9117" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "88" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126705} {:answer/id #uuid "58d9ec26-6711-5362-b07d-e834b12d650b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "112" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126706}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "df3fdcb9-dc5c-5e74-9f19-98291d28ee58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "64" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126703} :answer-field/id #uuid "40bac5a8-b668-596c-8096-2ac361a897eb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126701}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cea62eec-0c97-53ea-9e5d-d067ab50729f" :question/math-academy-id "q-162924" :question/problem "![](images/ff/ff6c3e308d89c9d07cff993f097fe2fcfafb22dab3399b9056f8db167b1b4b80.png)\n\nA plate occupies the region $D$ enclosed by the curves $y=2x$ and $y={x}^{2}$ in the first quadrant, as shown above. If the mass density function of $D$ is given by $\\lambda (x,y)=6x+14y,$ find the moment of inertia of the lamina about the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A={I}_{x}+{I}_{y}.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that $D$ is a type I region with representation\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\,{x}^{2}\\le y\\le 2x\\},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/fb/fb20e4c3052e28aeb2e6dad5054b0458db2d3b23d10991cfae27b5b00655a8fe.png)\n\nUsing the formula for ${I}_{y},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{y} & ={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{2x}{x}^{2}(6x+14y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{2x}6{x}^{3}+14{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[6{x}^{3}y+7{x}^{2}{y}^{2}]_{y={x}^{2}}^{y=2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(12{x}^{4}+28{x}^{4})-(6{x}^{5}+7{x}^{6})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}40{x}^{4}-6{x}^{5}-7{x}^{6}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =[8{x}^{5}-{x}^{6}-{x}^{7}]_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =256-64-128 \\\\  & =64.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129535 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129284 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "89f280af-9947-5d54-9284-9ae75d46c682" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Moment of Inertia Given a Description of a Mass Density Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0fe52c0a-9e8d-5b4f-99c3-86f55225cf03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{20M}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278732} {:answer/id #uuid "558e7a6e-54fa-5cf5-b950-f4588987db71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{18M}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279374} {:answer/id #uuid "6e7a5abc-84ff-5f6c-a768-c87b8daaf933" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15M}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279596} {:answer/id #uuid "8278e096-7229-5cc7-8e98-ca881cad0f81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16M}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279760} {:answer/id #uuid "9ed658dc-c3a3-5271-904b-ccb211ce8bcc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16M}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280002}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "558e7a6e-54fa-5cf5-b950-f4588987db71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{18M}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279374} :answer-field/id #uuid "74d4dcbe-c022-55cf-9e37-929259d9b865" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281047}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "54c0ec35-7150-5d58-acd5-421129a7e7ba" :question/math-academy-id "q-162986" :question/problem "A rectangular plate of mass $M$ occupies the region\n$D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 3\\}.$\nThe mass density at each point is proportional to the square of its distance from the $y$-axis.\nFind the moment of inertia of the lamina about the origin." :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A={I}_{x}+{I}_{y}.\n$$\n\nFrom the description, the mass density function can be written as $\\lambda (x,y)=k{x}^{2},$ where $k$ is a constant of proportionality. We can find an expression for $k$ in terms of $M$ by integrating the density function over the plate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}k{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =k({\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x)({\\int }_{0}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y) \\\\  & =k{[\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}]}_{0}^{1}\\cdot [y]_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =k(\\frac{1}{3}-0)(3-0) \\\\  & =k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $k=M$ and hence $\\lambda (x,y)=k{x}^{2}=M{x}^{2}.$\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the origin is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{0} & ={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =M{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\cdot {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =M{\\int }_{0}^{3}[{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{4}+{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & =M{\\int }_{0}^{3}{[\\frac{1}{5}{x}^{5}+\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}{y}^{2}]}_{x=0}^{x=1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =M{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{1}{5}+\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =M{[\\frac{1}{5}y+\\frac{1}{9}{y}^{3}]}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =M(\\frac{3}{5}+3-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{18M}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129537 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129285 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eba1f811-7b7d-56f6-8b49-4e7a4c7db81b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Moments of Inertia Using Polar Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "80de4587-dcf9-5d8e-b56f-181dbf1054a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7M}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126720} {:answer/id #uuid "e4a24dd0-408f-55e9-b605-390bb8a9c014" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7M}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126721} {:answer/id #uuid "058d611c-3957-50eb-a39a-d6ad31b6422b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9M}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126722} {:answer/id #uuid "6369fd64-4d73-5d21-97c2-0a0348fe4e7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4M" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126723} {:answer/id #uuid "67e212c8-9935-544e-a440-8c077803cbfb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9M}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126724}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "67e212c8-9935-544e-a440-8c077803cbfb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9M}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126724} :answer-field/id #uuid "ac852333-df04-5ad7-904c-15ecf5d8effb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126719}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f503a261-8e11-5e60-9902-aca301725e95" :question/math-academy-id "q-162988" :question/problem "![](images/82/82ed1d9c8965c445488256f2319bd2e1a25b2f685d5ad39c88f2b2ba7f7f5525.png)\n\nConsider a plate of mass $M$ that occupies the region $D$ in the first quadrant bounded by the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9,$ shown above. Given that the mass is distributed uniformly across the plate, find the moment of inertia of the plate about the origin." :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda (r,\\theta ),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $x$-axis, in polar coordinates, is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $y$-axis, in polar coordinates, is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin, in polar coordinates, is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ={I}_{x}+{I}_{y}.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that the region $D$ in polar coordinates can be expressed as\n\n$$\nD=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 3,\\,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nThe area of the plate $D$ is \n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{4}\\pi {r}^{2}=\\frac{9\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\n \n\nSince the mass is distributed uniformly, the mass density function is given by\n\n$$\n\\lambda (r,\\theta )=\\frac{M}{A}=\\frac{4M}{9\\pi }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the origin is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{0} & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{4M}{9\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{4M}{9\\pi }({\\int }_{0}^{3}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r)({\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{4M}{9\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}{r}^{4}{|}_{0}^{3}\\cdot \\theta {|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4M}{9\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}(81-0)\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{2}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{9M}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129538 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129285 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eba1f811-7b7d-56f6-8b49-4e7a4c7db81b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Moments of Inertia Using Polar Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3056ba5d-f7d1-55a3-8c91-5da94f695f90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9-4\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426091} {:answer/id #uuid "32392a3b-31a7-5a44-8746-b4c85858d143" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5-2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426092} {:answer/id #uuid "77d105e7-3ddf-576d-b7fd-6524428a349e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4-2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426108} {:answer/id #uuid "7faf887c-1e04-5044-ade7-d44593ea0768" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8-3\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426111} {:answer/id #uuid "d52f3d90-a693-584a-a56c-1a94156d2638" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10-4\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426136}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d52f3d90-a693-584a-a56c-1a94156d2638" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10-4\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426136} :answer-field/id #uuid "1239f0fe-7d29-5b4f-895a-1d599c74b28e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126725}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "41c034f9-14b4-5bc4-9351-1214f69c2032" :question/math-academy-id "q-162990" :question/problem "![](images/0a/0a85b0be5f8e5db2cf4d1716e4afe4a2780c500d94a1ce282a9d0044f93ba084.png)\n\nConsider the plate that occupies the region $D$ enclosed by the polar circles $r=1$ and $r=\\sqrt{3}$ and the polar rays $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ in the first quadrant, as shown above. The mass density function of the plate is given by $\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{6}{x}.$ Find the moment of inertia of the plate about the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda (r,\\theta ),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $x$-axis, in polar coordinates, is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $y$-axis, in polar coordinates, is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin, in polar coordinates, is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ={I}_{x}+{I}_{y}.\n$$\n\nFirst, note that the region $D$ in polar coordinates can be expressed as\n\n$$\nD=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,1\\le r\\le \\sqrt{3},\\,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{3}\\}.\n$$\n\nAlso, the mass density function in polar coordinates is \n\n$$\n\\lambda (r,\\theta )=\\frac{6}{r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }=\\frac{6}{r}\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{y} & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}{\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt{3}}(\\frac{6}{r}\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta ){r}^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}{\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt{3}}6{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =({\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt{3}}6{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r)({\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta ) \\\\  & =2{r}^{3}{|}_{1}^{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {|}_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =2(3\\sqrt{3}-1)\\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{3}-1}{2}) \\\\  & =(3\\sqrt{3}-1)\\cdot (\\sqrt{3}-1) \\\\  & =10-4\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426172 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129212 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3fdc1a2a-8a35-59f2-9890-5b835693df4f" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Moment of Inertia of a Lamina Given a Mass Density Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "080d8399-ca6e-5bfc-9948-10e6f55ec587" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.4\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426079} {:answer/id #uuid "52708feb-3913-5690-b2e1-2173e86cf70b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426102} {:answer/id #uuid "591fa420-a919-5ad2-a1d5-c2c1c7db9500" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.8\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426103} {:answer/id #uuid "9c33f085-6253-5add-a526-e7a9b5dd2021" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.2\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426126} {:answer/id #uuid "a184fae6-ae92-58da-b4e6-91168995a1c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426128}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9c33f085-6253-5add-a526-e7a9b5dd2021" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.2\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426126} :answer-field/id #uuid "6289bd5c-72da-5cc5-87b8-932a6822cc65" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426152}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d5a11202-6be7-5636-bb7a-f06e82bc2539" :question/math-academy-id "q-163145" :question/problem "A rectangular plate occupies the region\n$D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,2\\le x\\le 4,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 1\\}$\nwith mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)=x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{kg}/{\\text{m}}^{2},$ where $x$ and $y$ area measured in meters. Find the moment of inertia of this lamina about the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A={I}_{x}+{I}_{y}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for ${I}_{x},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{x} & ={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{2}^{4}{y}^{2}\\cdot x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{2}^{4}x{y}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{4}{[\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}]}_{2}^{4}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}6{y}^{4}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{6}{5}{y}^{5}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{5}-0 \\\\  & =1.2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia of the lamina about the $x$-axis is $1.2\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426173 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129212 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3fdc1a2a-8a35-59f2-9890-5b835693df4f" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Moment of Inertia of a Lamina Given a Mass Density Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "192a7bb2-a35e-573a-b092-31cfb1ddac02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426081} {:answer/id #uuid "2fe8041f-89cd-55a1-bc49-ec71764fdc11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9.5\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426090} {:answer/id #uuid "3ff64439-82c0-5c14-a157-cb5ff22cd101" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7.5\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426099} {:answer/id #uuid "d81ddfbe-fabd-5857-94dd-85908eecdf7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426137} {:answer/id #uuid "e99d02d8-a5b1-559f-9157-7617c73db56b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426141}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3ff64439-82c0-5c14-a157-cb5ff22cd101" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7.5\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426099} :answer-field/id #uuid "2d93a466-0910-583e-9e50-0cae93df198c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426148}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e2c7c1bb-fa37-5eb7-bb09-c424d91d3ac4" :question/math-academy-id "q-163147" :question/problem "A rectangular plate occupies the region\n$D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,e\\le x\\le {e}^{3},\\,\\,1\\le y\\le 2\\}$\nwith mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{y}{x}\\,\\text{kg}/{\\text{m}}^{2},$ where $x$ and $y$ area measured in meters. Find the moment of inertia of this lamina about the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A={I}_{x}+{I}_{y}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for ${I}_{x},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{x} & ={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{e}^{{e}^{3}}{y}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{y}{x}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}[{\\int }_{e}^{{e}^{3}}\\frac{{y}^{3}}{x}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{y}^{3}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡x]_{e}^{{e}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}2{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{y}^{4}}{2}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =8-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =7.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia of the lamina about the $x$-axis is $7.5\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426171 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129212 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3fdc1a2a-8a35-59f2-9890-5b835693df4f" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Moment of Inertia of a Lamina Given a Mass Density Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0028cd5f-1e12-5c9e-a228-965099fee635" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "64" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426078} {:answer/id #uuid "0994a76c-ed5e-593a-894e-4f2204ff3abf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426080} {:answer/id #uuid "4ef1ca64-5df4-54c2-97e4-48430b8722f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "56" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426101} {:answer/id #uuid "bfd9adf0-cc08-567c-aa22-06cfbd8dfcbd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "58" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426133} {:answer/id #uuid "e2e718aa-41b8-5ca7-8ebc-dc815f9ad555" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "42" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426140}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4ef1ca64-5df4-54c2-97e4-48430b8722f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "56" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426101} :answer-field/id #uuid "cdcb7809-2b77-53c7-8d22-ead9cc953f75" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426158}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c8a5bd24-d88b-5487-ab23-00b382428ad5" :question/math-academy-id "q-163175" :question/problem "![](images/eb/eb4ea276f4b876ce42b3701d60c1a5967769b62b328c16e9f9e75ef050f18d6f.png)\n\nA plate occupies the region $D$ enclosed by the $y$-axis and the curves $y={x}^{2}$ and $y=x+2$ in the first quadrant, as shown above. If the mass density function of $D$ is given by $\\lambda (x,y)=15x,$ find the moment of inertia of the lamina about the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A={I}_{x}+{I}_{y}.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that $D$ is a type I region with representation\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\,{x}^{2}\\le y\\le x+2\\},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/f1/f11f3026c9cc7f315781470791f03d3df97d7f292f8b713a1903f021bd5bf6c6.png)\n\nUsing the formula for ${I}_{y},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{y} & ={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{x+2}{x}^{2}(15x)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{x+2}15{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}15{x}^{3}[y]_{y={x}^{2}}^{y=x+2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}15{x}^{3}(x+2-{x}^{2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}15{x}^{4}+30{x}^{3}-15{x}^{5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[3{x}^{5}+\\frac{15}{2}{x}^{4}-\\frac{5}{2}{x}^{6}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =96+120-160 \\\\  & =56.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426162 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129284 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "89f280af-9947-5d54-9284-9ae75d46c682" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Moment of Inertia Given a Description of a Mass Density Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c2f23ac-51be-5a48-810d-c8379ed6f0fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7M}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426084} {:answer/id #uuid "9094cc57-8340-583e-95af-379689c091db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11M}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426121} {:answer/id #uuid "c49ccab0-35ec-5ecf-a625-b66e9da4db29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3M}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426134} {:answer/id #uuid "f238fa8a-6c9a-5483-86e7-4b21d4bed2c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5M}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426144} {:answer/id #uuid "fd45623e-0a2e-57e8-9377-05f2936d0a7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7M}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426147}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fd45623e-0a2e-57e8-9377-05f2936d0a7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7M}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426147} :answer-field/id #uuid "cd0f20a6-46f9-5205-a564-c57c64d1c959" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426157}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "288539c8-32f9-52b9-b8ac-741c3164b236" :question/math-academy-id "q-163176" :question/problem "A rectangular plate of mass $M$ occupies the region\n$D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 1\\}.$\nThe mass density at each point is proportional to the square of its distance from the origin.\nFind the moment of inertia of the lamina about the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A={I}_{x}+{I}_{y}.\n$$\n\nFrom the description, the mass density function can be written as $\\lambda (x,y)=k({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}),$ where $k$ is a constant of proportionality. We can find an expression for $k$ in terms of $M$ by integrating the density function over the plate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}k({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =k{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =k{\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}+x{y}^{2}]}_{x=0}^{x=1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =k{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{3}+{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =k{[\\frac{1}{3}y+\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =k(\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2k}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $k=\\frac{3M}{2}$ and hence,\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x,y)=k({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=\\frac{3M}{2}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{y} & ={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{3M}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}\\cdot ({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{3M}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{4}+{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{3M}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{1}{5}{x}^{5}+\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}{y}^{2}]}_{x=0}^{x=1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{3M}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{5}+\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{3M}{2}{[\\frac{1}{5}y+\\frac{1}{9}{y}^{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3M}{2}(\\frac{1}{5}+\\frac{1}{9}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3M}{2}\\cdot \\frac{14}{45} \\\\  & =\\frac{7M}{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426161 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129284 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "89f280af-9947-5d54-9284-9ae75d46c682" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Moment of Inertia Given a Description of a Mass Density Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23bbe7e1-a0cc-5c25-ab11-08a6c5e241a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5M}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426088} {:answer/id #uuid "3bf55755-4aed-5d41-88a5-9c0a40b76ff2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7M}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426096} {:answer/id #uuid "775fe094-a717-571b-bd6f-fac3a15f6c76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{M}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426107} {:answer/id #uuid "784cf5c3-1fe5-599a-bb3f-8487af249d36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9M}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426109} {:answer/id #uuid "905b1374-3ac8-5d0c-8955-bc6bb4f31bed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3M}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426120}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "905b1374-3ac8-5d0c-8955-bc6bb4f31bed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3M}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426120} :answer-field/id #uuid "85267851-4c38-5ca5-80e2-b8c0405c101b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426155}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "12797033-8f7a-5550-a318-a8461c5f9eb7" :question/math-academy-id "q-163177" :question/problem "A rectangular plate of mass $M$ occupies the region\n$D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 1\\}.$\nThe mass density at each point is proportional to the square of its distance from the $x$-axis.\nFind the moment of inertia of the lamina about the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A={I}_{x}+{I}_{y}.\n$$\n\nFrom the description, the mass density function can be written as $\\lambda (x,y)=k{y}^{2},$ where $k$ is a constant of proportionality. We can find an expression for $k$ in terms of $M$ by integrating the density function over the plate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}k{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =k({\\int }_{0}^{2}\\text{d}x)({\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y) \\\\  & =k[x]_{0}^{2}\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =k(2-0)(\\frac{1}{3}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{2k}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $k=\\frac{3M}{2}$ and hence,\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x,y)=k{y}^{2}=\\frac{3M}{2}{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{x} & ={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{3M}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{y}^{2}\\cdot {y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{3M}{2}({\\int }_{0}^{2}\\text{d}x)({\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{4}\\,\\text{d}y) \\\\  & =\\frac{3M}{2}[x]_{0}^{2}\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{5}{y}^{5}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3M}{2}(2-0)(\\frac{1}{5}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{3M}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367839 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129284 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "89f280af-9947-5d54-9284-9ae75d46c682" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Moment of Inertia Given a Description of a Mass Density Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5e0e4dc6-cc83-5e7f-b79b-720783cf7352" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5M}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367841} {:answer/id #uuid "a0587d1f-391c-5f9b-b8d8-5028a4784a60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "M" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367842} {:answer/id #uuid "4546c8b6-bbbc-54a2-93a7-576b3f98f83a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5M" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367843} {:answer/id #uuid "5adb24a3-dc97-532e-a764-ed7f738bd46a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2M" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367844} {:answer/id #uuid "ca913f2f-94bb-5779-968a-0f4050cccdc8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2M}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367845}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5e0e4dc6-cc83-5e7f-b79b-720783cf7352" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5M}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367841} :answer-field/id #uuid "836bf381-5e9c-5e6d-b2b3-b979431c05d9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367840}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "277b81af-ece5-57d8-a083-94f5105fa453" :question/math-academy-id "q-163181" :question/problem "A rectangular plate of mass $M$ occupies the region\n$D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 2\\}.$\nThe mass density at each point is proportional to the sum of its distance from the \n$x$-axis and its distance from the $y$-axis. Find the moment of inertia of the lamina about the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A={I}_{x}+{I}_{y}.\n$$\n\nFrom the description, the mass density function can be written as\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x,y)=k(|x|+|y|)=k(x+y),\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is a constant of proportionality. We can find an expression for $k$ in terms of $M$ by integrating the density function over the plate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}k(x+y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =k{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}x+y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =k{\\int }_{0}^{2}{[\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+xy]}_{x=0}^{x=2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =k{\\int }_{0}^{2}2+2y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =k{[2y+{y}^{2}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =k(4+4) \\\\  & =8k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $k=\\frac{M}{8}$ and hence,\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x,y)=k(x+y)=\\frac{M}{8}(x+y).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{x} & ={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{8}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{y}^{2}\\cdot (x+y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{8}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}x{y}^{2}+{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{8}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{[\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+x{y}^{3}]}_{x=0}^{x=2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{8}{\\int }_{0}^{2}2{y}^{2}+2{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{8}{[\\frac{2}{3}{y}^{3}+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{4}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{8}(\\frac{16}{3}+8) \\\\  & =\\frac{5M}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426170 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129284 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "89f280af-9947-5d54-9284-9ae75d46c682" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Moment of Inertia Given a Description of a Mass Density Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f77656b-3e45-51e6-975e-6c7b96eab0a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{M}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426097} {:answer/id #uuid "a5493aee-297c-543f-adfb-5eec085545c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6M" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426129} {:answer/id #uuid "a6c150b2-2390-546d-b4e7-66062a2c4005" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2M}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426130} {:answer/id #uuid "de5c60fb-e2bc-544b-a9ca-a8d623aefc96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3M}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426139} {:answer/id #uuid "efd5635a-4cdf-5391-80aa-6372607ddbb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2M" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426143}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a6c150b2-2390-546d-b4e7-66062a2c4005" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2M}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426130} :answer-field/id #uuid "3a42883f-5b06-58f7-ac0a-77d6e829d696" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426149}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c54dac84-cf20-51e9-9f3f-0a76f67b2f7b" :question/math-academy-id "q-163190" :question/problem "![](images/e8/e818c7ab51d26b642039c4f5e5157dee97772eab41b130ff8c9243f102c5fa20.png)\n\nA triangular plate of mass $M$ occupies the region $D$ in the first quadrant enclosed by the lines $y=3x,$ $x=1,$ and the $x$-axis. The mass density at each point is proportional to the square of its distance from the origin. Find the moment of inertia of the plate about the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A={I}_{x}+{I}_{y}.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that $D$ is a type I region with representation\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 3x\\},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/b7/b7457bee1577c81fc5c04d4b1a116e82790d7d251dc61bd0611d00aae7d34bfe.png)\n\nFrom the description, the mass density function can be written as $\\lambda (x,y)=k({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}),$ where $k$ is a constant of proportionality. We can find an expression for $k$ in terms of $M$ by integrating the density function over the plate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{3x}k({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =k{\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{3x}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & =k{\\int }_{0}^{1}{[{x}^{2}y+\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{3}]}_{y=0}^{y=3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =k{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}(3x)+\\frac{1}{3}(3x)^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =k{\\int }_{0}^{1}12{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =k{[3{x}^{4}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =3k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $k=\\frac{M}{3}$ and hence,\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x,y)=k({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=\\frac{M}{3}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{y} & ={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{3x}{x}^{2}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{3x}{x}^{4}+{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{[{x}^{4}y+\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{2}{y}^{3}]}_{y=0}^{y=3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{4}(3x)+\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{2}(3x)^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}12{x}^{5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{3}{[2{x}^{6}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2M}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426167 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129212 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3fdc1a2a-8a35-59f2-9890-5b835693df4f" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Moment of Inertia of a Lamina Given a Mass Density Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "212cb4ac-b452-5d7c-895a-ebccb8fb552a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426085} {:answer/id #uuid "71f8202f-2dfa-54c8-92d2-f18784e63f6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426105} {:answer/id #uuid "8c555f4c-2a3c-5935-8323-5b99c7dd42d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426117} {:answer/id #uuid "903a28aa-78bc-5cbd-9a20-130bf4ef78df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{32}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426119} {:answer/id #uuid "994061af-e142-509d-aef6-7a31cec161f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426124}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "903a28aa-78bc-5cbd-9a20-130bf4ef78df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{32}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426119} :answer-field/id #uuid "fe230e80-9c4a-5f11-8af8-1f23153eddbf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426160}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "780eb3bd-e477-5b2c-a046-3471aa40cca0" :question/math-academy-id "q-163195" :question/problem "![](images/1b/1bed15fe8f829f2a2248d5f366e4ca5df84274a767add87b3d26da9d3c2ab12d.png)\n\nA plate occupies the region $D$ enclosed by the $x$-axis and the lines $y=x$ and $y=4-x$, as shown above. If the mass density function of $D$ is given by $\\lambda (x,y)=2x,$ find the moment of inertia of the lamina about the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A={I}_{x}+{I}_{y}.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that $D$ is a type II region with representation\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,y\\le x\\le 4-y,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 2\\},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/fe/fee47e2abe427ce056c77adb5df9a003263bade6e6e3e8d62ca789c162e2348c.png)\n\nUsing the formula for ${I}_{x},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{x} & ={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{y}^{4-y}{y}^{2}\\cdot 2x\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{y}^{4-y}2x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[{x}^{2}{y}^{2}]_{x=y}^{x=4-y}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{y}^{2}(4-y)^{2}-{y}^{4}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}16{y}^{2}-8{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{16}{3}{y}^{3}-2{y}^{4}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{128}{3}-32 \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281631 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129211 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "92cc3d27-0e31-571f-9b92-4f209c5a08e0" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Moment of Inertia of a Lamina With Uniform Mass Density"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10a700cc-9a80-52fe-b948-d035b0b0b50c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6M\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278741} {:answer/id #uuid "365b10c1-9a22-5c79-b9b5-50173e8aadde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{10}{3}\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279066} {:answer/id #uuid "93ceeab5-b213-5adc-ad04-95c2bdacb462" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8M}{9}\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279906} {:answer/id #uuid "97708c84-8902-5f55-921b-48e526407aec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12M\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279931} {:answer/id #uuid "c877bf68-98d8-5d6e-ba06-a282245771bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10M\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280346}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c877bf68-98d8-5d6e-ba06-a282245771bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10M\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280346} :answer-field/id #uuid "5e2bfd6e-e15f-531c-8cb4-6aaab1b19f82" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280998}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f8b4a4d7-db6d-5f9c-bc7b-f800dc788f58" :question/math-academy-id "q-163200" :question/problem "A rectangular plate of mass $M\\,\\text{kg}$ and uniform mass density occupies the region\n$D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 3,\\,\\,1\\le y\\le 4\\},$ where $x$ and $y$ are measured in meters. Find the moment of inertia of the plate about the origin." :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A={I}_{x}+{I}_{y}.\n$$\n\nThe area of the rectangular plate $D$ is $A=3\\cdot 3=9.$ \n\nSince the plate has uniform density, every unit of area contains the same amount of mass. Therefore, the mass density is constant and is given by the total mass divided by the total area:\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{M}{A}=\\frac{M}{9}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for ${I}_{0},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{0} & ={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{9}{\\int }_{1}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{9}{\\int }_{1}^{4}{[\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}+x{y}^{2}]}_{x=0}^{x=3}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{9}{\\int }_{1}^{4}9+3{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{9}{[9y+{y}^{3}]}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{9}(36+64-9-1) \\\\  & =10M\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia is $10M\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426169 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129211 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "92cc3d27-0e31-571f-9b92-4f209c5a08e0" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Moment of Inertia of a Lamina With Uniform Mass Density"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21e7bd91-295b-518a-b561-81121cd85323" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{10M}{3}\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426086} {:answer/id #uuid "3b931fa2-83ac-51fe-bb7c-19a20e4185e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7M}{3}\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426095} {:answer/id #uuid "3ff58e4c-ec26-5573-9e2f-2d04c31bdf0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14M\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426098} {:answer/id #uuid "4723d509-fb10-5fce-b47c-17128e37c80a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7M\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426100} {:answer/id #uuid "68bfb188-a994-53ac-b526-94cd37c66536" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4M}{3}\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426104}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3b931fa2-83ac-51fe-bb7c-19a20e4185e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7M}{3}\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426095} :answer-field/id #uuid "d64a47db-1f98-5942-95f8-0a5fe8a1d806" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426159}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a576d72d-3059-5635-bf8f-38bb7570610e" :question/math-academy-id "q-163202" :question/problem "A rectangular plate of mass $M\\,\\text{kg}$ and uniform mass density occupies the region\n$D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 3\\},$ where $x$ and $y$ are measured in meters. Find the moment of inertia of the plate about the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A={I}_{x}+{I}_{y}.\n$$\n\nThe area of the rectangular plate $D$ is $A=1\\cdot 3=3.$ \n\nSince the plate has uniform density, every unit of area contains the same amount of mass. Therefore, the mass density is constant and is given by the total mass divided by the total area:\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{M}{A}=\\frac{M}{3}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for ${I}_{y},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{y} & ={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{[\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}]}_{1}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{7}{3}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{7M}{9}[y]_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{7M}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia is $\\frac{7M}{3}\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426168 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129211 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "92cc3d27-0e31-571f-9b92-4f209c5a08e0" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Moment of Inertia of a Lamina With Uniform Mass Density"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a308805-aad8-54a7-b5b1-064462e81b88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{22M}{3}\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426082} {:answer/id #uuid "383fad8b-59a8-5b43-ad3d-50f07ba39230" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426094} {:answer/id #uuid "7cd77a93-c9ac-5031-8c15-eb3b4d405d24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "26M\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426110} {:answer/id #uuid "b11137df-53c8-5030-b8e0-6a9bb71fdcb0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16M}{3}\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426131} {:answer/id #uuid "f370ad7c-77cf-51a3-8d2a-a9e647d5cb34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13M}{3}\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426145}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f370ad7c-77cf-51a3-8d2a-a9e647d5cb34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13M}{3}\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426145} :answer-field/id #uuid "73ed7367-47d9-5ab5-97c8-cd0c7ced1597" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426153}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a005e400-255d-52bf-b2df-866a68951503" :question/math-academy-id "q-163203" :question/problem "A rectangular plate of mass $M\\,\\text{kg}$ and uniform mass density occupies the region\n$D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le 4,\\,\\,1\\le y\\le 3\\},$ where $x$ and $y$ are measured in meters. Find the moment of inertia of the plate about the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A={I}_{x}+{I}_{y}.\n$$\n\nThe area of the rectangular plate $D$ is $A=3\\cdot 2=6.$ \n\nSince the plate has uniform density, every unit of area contains the same amount of mass. Therefore, the mass density is constant and is given by the total mass divided by the total area:\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{M}{A}=\\frac{M}{6}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for ${I}_{x},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{x} & ={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{6}{\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{1}^{4}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{6}{\\int }_{1}^{3}{y}^{2}[x]_{1}^{4}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{6}{\\int }_{1}^{3}3{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{6}{[{y}^{3}]}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{6}(27-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{13M}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia is $\\frac{13M}{3}\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426166 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129211 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "92cc3d27-0e31-571f-9b92-4f209c5a08e0" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Moment of Inertia of a Lamina With Uniform Mass Density"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a8aba61-7fbb-51be-b039-d908378043a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2M" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426089} {:answer/id #uuid "76eeb049-de78-53bc-bb34-66955cbadcbb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{M}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426106} {:answer/id #uuid "81ded899-4980-59c2-af9d-b7df5118b7b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3M" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426112} {:answer/id #uuid "9993ebf7-725c-5fea-8632-8edbaaabad83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2M}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426125} {:answer/id #uuid "f5f49ecb-bfb7-5254-96e9-80b6a04204b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7M}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426146}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f5f49ecb-bfb7-5254-96e9-80b6a04204b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7M}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426146} :answer-field/id #uuid "4ff40cde-4235-5812-940d-6ae7a7815ea3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426150}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6c69411e-7617-5386-8531-531c78fc6d71" :question/math-academy-id "q-163214" :question/problem "![](images/75/7545f5c48a86aa8ab7a8f46272e01f3021b7d7be10eea2019ce31c3225ca2f4d.png)\n\nA triangular plate of mass $M$ and uniform mass density occupies the region $D$ enclosed between the $x$-axis and the lines $y=x$ and $y=2-x,$ as shown above. Find the moment of inertia of the plate about the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A={I}_{x}+{I}_{y}.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that $D$ is a type II region with representation\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,\\,y\\le x\\le 2-y\\},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/b1/b1ced30e4cc1fe984b56707f11e8720f2ee68c5b204b6ea7b8d607a65276acca.png)\n\nThe area of the triangular plate $D$ is \n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot 1=1.\n$$\n\nSince the plate has uniform density, every unit of area contains the same amount of mass. Therefore, the mass density is constant and is given by the total mass divided by the total area:\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{M}{A}=M.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{y} & ={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =M{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{y}^{2-y}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =M{\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}]}_{y}^{2-y}\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2-y)^{3}-{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{3}{[-\\frac{1}{4}(2-y)^{4}-\\frac{1}{4}{y}^{4}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{M}{12}{[(2-y)^{4}+{y}^{4}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{M}{12}(1+1-16-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{7M}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426165 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129285 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eba1f811-7b7d-56f6-8b49-4e7a4c7db81b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Moments of Inertia Using Polar Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ae42d1a-6d46-5f68-a58f-63f3f01ba083" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "M" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426083} {:answer/id #uuid "84a648d6-9b98-5e2b-96c3-2d8410a9822a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2M" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426114} {:answer/id #uuid "8e598212-d811-50b7-bec5-2b0589d8de38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{M}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426118} {:answer/id #uuid "b95793fa-2522-5d8c-bf1a-4e74f2539faf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4M" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426132} {:answer/id #uuid "dd025262-3d7b-5e61-b549-0d29e583e1df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{M}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426138}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8e598212-d811-50b7-bec5-2b0589d8de38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{M}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426118} :answer-field/id #uuid "9936f427-3e8b-5ed5-aa88-8837211a03b0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426156}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6773c2a6-0930-567e-b7b2-ce599d9b40d9" :question/math-academy-id "q-163244" :question/problem "![](images/cc/ccf2ad2b3d8b39dbacdcdce6bd317c5720ce3b750249f6c328e60d3c74ddb4ef.png)\n\nConsider a plate of mass $M$ that occupies the region $D$ in the lower half plane bounded by the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1,$ shown above. Given that the mass is distributed uniformly across the plate, find the moment of inertia of the plate about the origin." :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda (r,\\theta ),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $x$-axis, in polar coordinates, is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $y$-axis, in polar coordinates, is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin, in polar coordinates, is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ={I}_{x}+{I}_{y}.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that the region $D$ in polar coordinates can be expressed as\n\n$$\nD=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 1,\\,\\,\\pi \\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nThe area of the plate $D$ is \n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}\\pi {r}^{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\n \n\nSince the mass is distributed uniformly, the mass density function is given by\n\n$$\n\\lambda (r,\\theta )=\\frac{M}{A}=\\frac{2M}{\\pi }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the origin is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{0} & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{2M}{\\pi }{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{2M}{\\pi }({\\int }_{0}^{1}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r)({\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }\\text{d}\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{2M}{\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}{r}^{4}\\,{|}_{0}^{1}\\cdot \\theta \\,{|}_{\\pi }^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{2M}{\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426164 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129285 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eba1f811-7b7d-56f6-8b49-4e7a4c7db81b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Moments of Inertia Using Polar Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "82f8d43e-1035-5a1c-af08-cbdb74b8e521" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{25M}{18}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426113} {:answer/id #uuid "90d33d86-42b4-50b5-b225-bc12cdb4cce2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{17M}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426122} {:answer/id #uuid "94f6f90f-22ba-547f-b70c-dec53052cc53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13M}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426123} {:answer/id #uuid "9d138ce3-cd8e-580f-8cc9-f6a4bd9331f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{23M}{14}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426127} {:answer/id #uuid "ebdab63f-5d26-54ff-826e-4d5769b5c027" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{27M}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426142}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "94f6f90f-22ba-547f-b70c-dec53052cc53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13M}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426123} :answer-field/id #uuid "57737971-0b74-5a3a-9e35-ebe85b625ce1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426151}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "648c554b-c03a-5281-b06a-dd87f9becdf0" :question/math-academy-id "q-163250" :question/problem "![](images/52/52a326d518bdbe32f119d131e67ae6eb35a3c72479c7ccb5a35dae030d6b9e1c.png)\n\nConsider a plate of mass $M$ that occupies the region $D$ in the second quadrant bounded by the polar circles $r=1,$ $r=\\sqrt{3},$ and the coordinate axes, as shown above. The mass density at any point is proportional to the square of its distance from the origin. Find the moment of inertia of the plate about the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda (r,\\theta ),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $x$-axis, in polar coordinates, is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $y$-axis, in polar coordinates, is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin, in polar coordinates, is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ={I}_{x}+{I}_{y}.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that the region $D,$ in polar coordinates, can be expressed as\n\n$$\nD=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,1\\le r\\le \\sqrt{3},\\,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nFrom the description, the mass density function can be written as $\\lambda (r,\\theta )=k{r}^{2},$ where $k$ is a constant of proportionality. We can find an expression for $k$ in terms of $M$ by integrating the density function over the plate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt{3}}k{r}^{2}\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =k{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt{3}}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =k({\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt{3}}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r)({\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\text{d}\\theta ) \\\\  & =k\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}{r}^{4}\\,{|}_{1}^{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot \\theta \\,{|}_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =k\\cdot 2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\  & =\\pi k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\lambda (r,\\theta )=k{r}^{2}=\\frac{M}{\\pi }{r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{x} & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{\\pi }{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt{3}}{r}^{2}\\cdot {r}^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{\\pi }{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt{3}}{r}^{5}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{\\pi }({\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt{3}}{r}^{5}\\,\\text{d}r)({\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{\\pi }({\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt{3}}{r}^{5}\\,\\text{d}r)({\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}{r}^{6}\\,{|}_{1}^{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}\\theta -\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta ){|}_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{13}{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{13M}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426163 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129285 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eba1f811-7b7d-56f6-8b49-4e7a4c7db81b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Moments of Inertia Using Polar Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22b4eab4-e67f-5253-a859-3498612df35b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{119M}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426087} {:answer/id #uuid "33fd7f69-69b9-5e20-a9bf-e30e6305ddd5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{633M}{95}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426093} {:answer/id #uuid "897c8c9c-3a68-592d-8474-dcf1e5940faf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{67M}{20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426115} {:answer/id #uuid "8add3412-3a15-5d8e-8ab9-c3dd9138ea4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{92M}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426116} {:answer/id #uuid "ce7a4d21-3102-5b15-ad17-f21cd1da939e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{243M}{32}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426135}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "33fd7f69-69b9-5e20-a9bf-e30e6305ddd5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{633M}{95}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426093} :answer-field/id #uuid "7d474c07-7cb2-5ef8-ac28-8b48fcc0689f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426154}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "40276498-22d3-5917-98ab-3914446aeb38" :question/math-academy-id "q-163253" :question/problem "![](images/74/74ae29ecab0a2d87fb020cc0801277a4a08dd11d38ea2b81f0718c6d30a19598.png)\n\nConsider a plate of mass $M$ that occupies the region $D$ bounded by the polar circles $r=2$ and $r=3,$ as shown above. The mass density at any point is proportional to its distance from the origin. Find the moment of inertia of the plate about the origin." :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda (r,\\theta ),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $x$-axis, in polar coordinates, is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $y$-axis, in polar coordinates, is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin, in polar coordinates, is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ={I}_{x}+{I}_{y}.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that the region $D,$ in polar coordinates, can be expressed as\n\n$$\nD=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,2\\le r\\le 3,\\,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nFrom the description, the mass density function can be written as $\\lambda (r,\\theta )=kr,$ where $k$ is a constant of proportionality. We can find an expression for $k$ in terms of $M$ by integrating the density function over the plate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{2}^{3}kr\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =k{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{2}^{3}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =k({\\int }_{2}^{3}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r)({\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\text{d}\\theta ) \\\\  & =k\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}{r}^{3}\\,{|}_{2}^{3}\\cdot \\theta \\,{|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =k\\cdot \\frac{19}{3}\\cdot 2\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{38\\pi k}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\lambda (r,\\theta )=kr=\\frac{3M}{38\\pi }r.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the origin is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{0} & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{3M}{38\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{2}^{3}r\\cdot {r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{3M}{38\\pi }({\\int }_{2}^{3}{r}^{4}\\,\\text{d}r)({\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\text{d}\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{3M}{38\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}{r}^{5}\\,{|}_{2}^{3}\\cdot \\theta \\,{|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{3M}{38\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{211}{5}\\cdot 2\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{633M}{95}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
